# Git lab situations (Software Engineer track)

Apply when writing or rewriting pack `description`, `overview`, `brief`, hints, or solution copy for Forgeboard Git labs. Audience is beginners learning one Git mechanic; story stays inside Forgeboard.

Companion: blogs use `.cursor/rules/git-blogs.mdc`. World source of truth: `backend/challenges/software-engineering/TRACK.md` (Subtrack 1 — Git).

## World (constant across chapters)

- Product: **Forgeboard** (multi-tenant project tracker). Learner: **Ada Learner** on **Core**.
- Codebase: **`sprint-service`** at `~/sprint-service` (except git-01, which starts with clone).
- Team remote: bare forge at `learner@git.forgeboard.internal:...` (no Gitea / PR UI). Labs end at push / investigation where that is the workflow.
- Teammates who show up in copy: **Jordan** (tech lead), **Priya**, Ops, QA — use them as blockers or hand-offs, not as jargon dumpers.
- After git-01: forge **rejects direct pushes to `main`**. Learner opens a fix/feature branch, pushes that branch, leaves `main` alone (local and remote) unless the lab explicitly teaches otherwise.
- Prefer plain terms; define product words once when the lab depends on them (**staging**, **staging deploy**, **release/2.1**, health check). Do not redefine the whole company every lab.

## Chapter plots (keep constant within a chapter)

Each chapter is a **subplot**. Do not drag Ch1 branches into Ch2 or Ch3 stories. Within a chapter, later labs may continue the same thread (e.g. Ch1 `fix/agentic-default`).

| Ch | Theme | Story spine (almost constant) | Typical branches / artifacts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **1** Commits & branches | First ship + fix branch hygiene | Agentic-sprints **beta vs full**; bad global default; work on **`fix/agentic-default`** | `main`, `fix/agentic-default`, `.env.local` / amend tip |
| **2** Merge / rebase / cherry-pick | Sync features with moving main; ship a hotfix onto a freeze line | **Board summary**, **board filters**, **CSV export**; production hotfix onto **`release/2.1`** | `feature/board-summary`, `feature/board-filters`, `feature/csv-export`, `release/2.1` |
| **3** History & recovery | Undo / find / attribute mistakes without rewriting shared main | **Board notify**, **column auto-advance**, **Sprint Service health check** (`./check.sh`), batch multiplier | `fix/reenable-board-notify`, `feature/column-auto-advance`, `v1.0`, `~/answers/*` when reporting |
| **4** Collaboration | Shared feature + parallel hotfix | **Workspace invites** (TTL, redeem, docs) for stash / merge / capstone; **outbound webhooks** for worktree | `feature/workspace-invites`, `fix/invite-ttl-72`, `feature/webhook-delivery`, `fix/webhook-timeout-30` |
| **5** Releases, remotes, hooks | Tags, fork sync, hooks, scrub secrets | Release tags / fork **upstream** / ticket prefix / invite-redeem docs with leaked key | `v2.2.0`, `hotfix/2.1.1`, `fix/hook-ticket-prefix`, `feature/invite-redeem-docs` |

When editing one lab, skim TRACK.md for that chapter’s row so titles, branch names, and subplot do not drift.

## Pack `description` structure

Use this shape (same order every time):

```text
**SITUATION:**

<1–2 short paras: where we are in Forgeboard + what broke or what is blocked>
<optional: define one term the learner must understand (staging deploy, tag v1.0, …)>
<constraint: why not the naïve Git move (don’t push main, don’t delete the file, investigation only, …)>
<hand-off: who needs what from the learner>

Your identity (Ada Learner) is already configured. …   # omit on git-01 if identity is a task
Start on <branch> in ~/sprint-service.                 # or clone instructions for git-01

**YOUR TASK:**

1. …
2. …
…
```

### Situation rules

- **One job.** The situation exists so the Git mechanic is motivated — not to teach all of Forgeboard.
- **Earn constraints.** If the lab assumes “exactly one bad commit” or “investigation only,” say who decided that in one simple beat (e.g. Jordan skimmed and forgot to pin the SHA; handed you the follow-up). Do **not** invent a long forensics paragraph (`status.env`, deploy-log essays) unless the lab teaches that forensics.
- **Define only what the lab uses.** Staging vs production, what `./check.sh` means, what a tag is — once, in plain language. Skip chapter recaps (“you finished lab 2…”).
- **No spoiler pedagogy.** Don’t name the command in the situation when the task is to discover it (`git bisect`, `git blame`, `git rm --cached`) — put commands in hints / solution.
- **No meta lab talk.** Avoid “this lab,” “rescue,” “Quick branch map,” fake-key parentheticals, or “the grader will…”.
- **Backticks for copyable identifiers** (same blue chips as git-01 / git-02): branch names (`feature/…`, `fix/…`, `main`), paths (`~/sprint-service`, `src/…`, `config/…`), remotes (`origin`, `upstream`), tags, config keys, and answer files. Keep `**bold**` for conceptual emphasis (counts, “uncommitted”, product words) — do not bold branch/file names instead of ticking them.
- Prefer **2–4 task steps**. Combine ignore+untrack, or commit+push+end-state, when separate steps are only verify. Drop inspect-only steps if Situation already states the facts.
- End-state criteria belong in the last task (clean tree, file still on disk, leave main alone) — not a separate **SPEC:** block unless the lab family already uses SPEC consistently.
- Keep **overview** / **brief** aligned with Situation in one or two sentences; solution README should match the same plot beat.

### Task rules

- Numbered, actionable, ordered the way a careful learner would work.
- Explicit: leave `main` alone / do not rewrite shared history when that is required.
- **No literal commands in YOUR TASK.** Do not put `git status`, `git push -u …`, `git add …`, `git clone …`, `--force-with-lease`, etc. in task steps (even in parentheses). State the outcome (“push `fix/…` to `origin`”, “fold into the tip”). Put the exact invocation in **hints** / solution.
- Written answers go under `~/answers/<name>` only for labs that call `ensure_answers` (bisect SHA, blame email, optional cherry-pick SHA). Most Git labs have **no** answers folder — don’t invent one in copy.

## Voice checklist

```text
❌ Ops already confirmed the failure mode: exactly one commit… [long status.env / deploy-log proof]
✅ Jordan skimmed the range, is sure one commit spilled the break, forgot to pin it, handed you the follow-up

❌ That script is the board health check: exit 0 means boards look healthy…
✅ That script is the Sprint Service health check: exit 0 means the service looks healthy…

❌ (a fake key for this lab) / Leave main untouched — the team remote will not accept a push straight onto main.
✅ Say the constraint once in human terms; forge deny-main is enforced by setup — don’t lecture unless the beat needs it

❌ Five tasks: inspect, ignore, untrack, push, confirm clean
✅ Two tasks: ignore+untrack; commit+push+clean end state
```

## Preview before mechanics

When rewriting Situation/plot with the user: **preview full Situation + YOUR TASK first**. Do not change `setup.sh` / `grade.sh` until they accept the copy (unless they ask to apply everything).

## Examples (chapter spines)

- **Ch1 continue:** “You are continuing work on `fix/agentic-default`…” — same agentic default thread.
- **Ch2 new:** board summary / filters / CSV / `release/2.1` — do not mention `fix/agentic-default`.
- **Ch3 new:** notify / auto-advance / `./check.sh` / blame multiplier — do not reuse Ch2 feature names.
- **Ch4 new:** workspace invites — separate from notify subplot.
- **Ch5:** tags / upstream / hooks / scrub — release and remote mechanics on the same forge.
